  "text": "Solution 2 is more likely correct, though I'm working from memory and could not check the literature.\n\n- **Solution 1 (c = 1)** claims Katznelson proved that $o(\\log n)$ growth forces affineness. That is essentially Kahane's conjecture, which I believe is still open. Kahane's piecewise-linear examples show that non-affine $\\phi$ can reach $O(\\log n)$ growth, so exponent 1 is the conjectured optimum, not a proven theorem.\n- **Solution 2 (c = 1/2)** describes the classical Beurling–Helson theorem (bounded norms force affineness), Kahane's conjecture, and the optimality of exponent 1. It also cites a previous exponent of $1/22$ due to Lebedev and a quantitative improvement to $\\log^{1/2-\\varepsilon}$. I recall work by Lev and Matolcsi giving an exponent of this kind, but I cannot confirm the specific value from memory, so this is the weakest link.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
